DESCRIPTION:Clay Exploration/Handbuilding - 01/07-02/11/2027\n\n  \n    \n
       \n        \n          $255.00\n        \n      \n    \n  \n\nThursda
 ys\, January 7th - February 11th 2027 (6 sessions) 5:30-8:00pm$210 + $45 c
 lay fee Pre-Registration Required\, restricted to 12 students.Class Descri
 ption: This course is open to all and is designed for students wishing to 
 further develop hand-building techniques. The class will be tailored to st
 udents' individual skill levels with a goal to help them refine their hand
  building skills and learn new things. Each session will start with a dem
 onstration of a specific technique related to functional and/or decorative
  pottery. Students are invited to explore new ideas and to try more chall
 enging pieces in an educational and supportive setting. This class explore
 s freeform coil pots\, soft slab work\, hard slab technique\, sculptural t
 echniques\, and dabbles in the many stamping\, carving and textural surfac
 e treatments that can be used to decorate clay builds. We will investigate
  ways to use slips and glaze that will add color to the forms we create. 
 This class is held in the ClayGround at the Sebastopol Center for the Arts
  at 282 South High Street\, Sebastopol\, CA 95472.About the Teacher: Micha
 el Citrino has had a love affair with clay for over 50 years. After his co
 llege experience at California College of Arts and Crafts with Viola Frey 
 and further work at Humboldt State University with Lew Merrick\, he ran a 
 stained glass business for 18 years. Michael and his wife left for Turkey 
 in 1991 to teach in International Schools. For 26 years he taught K-12 art
 \, including Ceramics\, Drawing\, Photography\, Design and Sculpture at sc
 hools in six different countries. He started clay programs for High School
  and Middle School students at three of those schools. At four of these sc
 hools he offered after school and weekend ceramics sessions for teachers a
 nd parents. Michael’s work ranges from the traditional to the experiment
 al and a wide swath in between. His approach to teaching is to help studen
 ts explore the playful nature of the clay room and nurture a love for the 
 possibilities that medium presents.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\
